I came up with this:
```kotlin
class PlaybackStatsHandler(
    private val player: SimpleExoPlayer,
    private val queueRepository: QueueRepository
) {

    /**
     * This field is used as workaround for issue:
     * https://github.com/google/ExoPlayer/issues/7193
     * We can remove this after ExoPlayer 2.12 release
     */
    private var firstQueueItemInPlaylist: QueueItem? = null

    private var currentStatsListener: PlaybackStatsListener? = null

    fun startPlayback() {
        currentStatsListener?.let {
            it.finishAllSessions()
            player.removeAnalyticsListener(it)
        }
        val statsListener = createStatsListener()
        currentStatsListener = statsListener
        player.addAnalyticsListener(statsListener)
    }

    fun setFirstQueueItem(queueItem: QueueItem) {
        firstQueueItemInPlaylist = queueItem
    }

    private fun createStatsListener() = PlaybackStatsListener(true) { eventTime, playbackStats ->
        if (eventTime.timeline.isEmpty) {
            // this occurs for the first track in playlist. This is a known bug in ExoPlayer
            // https://github.com/google/ExoPlayer/issues/7193
            // We can remove this after ExoPlayer 2.12 release
            val firstQueueItem = firstQueueItemInPlaylist
            if (firstQueueItem != null) {
                Timber.d("[AnalyticsListener] Playback [${firstQueueItem.trackId}] - ${playbackStats.totalPlayTimeMs.getAsSeconds()}")
                firstQueueItemInPlaylist = null
            } else {
                Timber.e("Empty timeline without first queue item set")
            }
            return@PlaybackStatsListener
        }

        val tag = eventTime.timeline.getWindow(
            eventTime.windowIndex,
            Timeline.Window()
        ).tag as? MediaDescriptionCompat
        val id = tag?.mediaId?.toInt() ?: return@PlaybackStatsListener
        val queueItem = queueRepository.getQueueItemById(id) ?: return@PlaybackStatsListener
        Timber.d("[AnalyticsListener] Playback [${queueItem.trackId}] - ${playbackStats.totalPlayTimeMs.getAsSeconds()}")
    }
}
```
Method `startPlayback` is called before preparing new mediaSource. Method `setFirstQueueItem` is called in the moment i know which track is played first.

It doesn't crash and it sometimes gives a proper results. But, sometimes (seems like one in 3 trials) it reports zero time on playlist change.

Sometimes it works properly:
```
D/PlaybackStatsHandler$createStatsListener: [AnalyticsListener] Playback [65876] - 12
D/PlaybackRepository$reportPlayback: Playback [65876] - 12 <- **This is from our custom playback reporter which gives expected and proper result**
```

But sometimes it still returns "zero" time and empty timeline.

```
D/PlaybackStatsHandler$createStatsListener: [AnalyticsListener] Playback [8350] - 0
E/PlaybackStatsHandler$createStatsListener: Empty timeline without first queue item set
D/PlaybackRepository$reportPlayback: Playback [8350] - 13 <- **This is from our custom playback reporter which gives expected and proper result**
```

It seems like some race condition here, i'm still trying to locate probably cause on our side.
If it help - we are creating our media source like that (heavily redacted):

```kotlin
playbackStatsHandler.startPlayback()

val mediaSource = ConcatenatingMediaSource(false, true, DefaultShuffleOrder(0))

tracks.forEachIndexed { index, queueItem ->
    mediaSource.addMediaSource(
        mediaSourcePreparer.prepare(queueItem, useCache) // we have different strategies for multiple audio sources
    )
    if (index == startPosition) { // start position holds index we want to start playback on
        // start playback right away when position is ready
        exoPlayer.prepare(mediaSource)
        playbackStatsHandler.setFirstQueueItem(queueItem)
        try {
            exoPlayer.seekTo(index, 0)
        } catch (ex: IllegalSeekPositionException) {
            Timber.e(ex)
        }
    }
}
exoPlayer.playWhenReady = true
```

EDIT: i think this works properly if index startPosition is 0. If startPosition is different - magic is happening. Sometimes it gives proper time and sometimes it gives 0 or empty timeline. I cannot figure out pattern here
